WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:11.400
Today we are going to use an AI to fly&nbsp;
into this photo. And we will see how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.100
much better this technique has become&nbsp;
in just one year. It will be insanity.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.360
Yes, in a previous video, we explored an insane&nbsp;
idea: what if we could take just one photograph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:32.040
of a landscape, and then, we would fly into this&nbsp;
photo like a bird. Of course, that is a big ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:39.360
because to be able to do this, we would need to&nbsp;
invent at least 3 things. One is image inpainting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.720
Look, when we start flying, the regions between&nbsp;
the trees are missing. We need to generate those.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:54.000
Two, information is also missing not just within,&nbsp;
but around the photo. This is a huge problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.940
because completely new regions should&nbsp;
also appear that are beyond the image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.840
This means that we also need&nbsp;
to perform image outpainting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.880
creating these new regions from scratch.&nbsp;
Continuing the image, if you will.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.780
And three, as we fly closer to these new regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:19.020
we will be looking at fewer and fewer pixels&nbsp;
and from closer and closer, which means…this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:27.600
For this, we would need super resolution - in&nbsp;
goes a coarse image or video, and this AI-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.440
method is tasked with…this! Yes. This is not&nbsp;
science fiction. This is super resolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.680
where the AI starts out from noise and&nbsp;
synthesizes crisp details onto the image.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:47.160
So, last year, scientists at Google&nbsp;
created an amazing AI that was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.980
to learn and fuse all these three&nbsp;
techniques together, to create this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:02:00.600
Wow, so this is possible after all. Well, hold on&nbsp;
to your papers, because it is not only possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:07.440
but the followup paper is already here, we are&nbsp;
now one more paper down the line, in less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.740
a year later! I know you’re itching to see&nbsp;
it, me too, so let’s compare them together!

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:22.740
These methods will all start from the same point,&nbsp;
and oh my, look how quickly they deviate. The two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.260
earlier methods quickly break down, and this&nbsp;
is the work that we talked about a few weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:35.040
ago. This is clearly much better, however, as&nbsp;
every single one of you Fellow Scholars can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:42.600
it lacks temporal coherence. What is that?&nbsp;
Well, this means that the AI does not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:49.020
a long-term vision of what it wishes to do, and&nbsp;
barely remembers what it did just a few frames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:56.580
ago. As a result, these landscapes start morphing&nbsp;
into something completely different very quickly.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:03.540
And now, you know what’s coming, so hold onto&nbsp;
your papers and let's look at the new technique!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:10.140
My goodness, I love it! So much&nbsp;
improvement in just a year. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.680
everyone can see that these are also not perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.680
but this kind of improvement just one more&nbsp;
paper down the line is nothing short of amazing.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:27.960
Especially that it doesn’t only have better&nbsp;
quality. No-no, it can do even more. It offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:35.100
us more control too! Now we can turn the camera&nbsp;
around and whenever we see something interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.180
we can decide which direction we wish to go.&nbsp;
And the result is that now, we can create and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.480
also control these beautiful, long aerial&nbsp;
videos where after the first few frames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:55.680
every single thing is synthesized by an AI.&nbsp;
How cool is that? What a time to be alive!

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:02.280
And, it doesn’t stop there. It gets even better.&nbsp;
If you have been holding on to your papers so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:09.600
now squeeze that paper, because this new AI&nbsp;
synthesizes these videos…without ever having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:16.680
seen one. That’s right, it had never seen a video.&nbsp;
The previous work was trained on drone videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:22.020
but training this one only requires&nbsp;
a collection of single photographs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:28.800
Multiple views and the camera position&nbsp;
are not required. That is insanity. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.080
AI is so much smarter than the previous&nbsp;
one that was published just a year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.560
and it requires training data that is much&nbsp;
easier to produce at the same time. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.140
I wonder what we will be capable of just&nbsp;
two more papers down the line. So cool!

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.900
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

